esp32s31: esp32s31_pie_log - print the next N lent-CPU PIE traps (diagnostic)

Files (whole files): arch/riscv/kernel/esp32s31-ext.c.

core_param esp32s31_pie_log (0644, default 0). Writing N makes the next N
PIE traps on the lent CPU (Linux CPU1 = hart 0) print one line each:

  s31pie <comm>[<pid>] pc <epc> ra <ra> sp <sp> s0 <s0>: <8 user stack words>

and the value counts down to 0. pc/ra resolve against the process's maps
(an offset into libc.so or the binary). Nothing else changes: with the
parameter at 0 the trap path gains one READ_ONCE.

Built as kernel #394 (2026-09-26, images/diag-394-pielog-*) to find what
makes the glxgears fullscreen dips. It showed that musl's memcpy is a PIE
user too - libc+0x5894a (memcpy+0x4e) jal libc+0x107b0, a 128-bit
esp.vld/esp.vst copier placed after strcmp, taken from 64 bytes up - which
the "PIE in exactly four functions" note had missed; lvdesk and glxgears
both trapped there. See docs/gl-plan-2026-09-25.md (glxgears dips) and
lvdesk/lentcpu.c.

TRAP of the instrument itself: every line it prints is read by klogd and
written by syslogd, both static busybox with PIE string code, so on the lent
CPU they trap in turn and consume the budget (35 klogd + 26 syslogd of 101
lines in the first capture). Keep N small, or read the task names first.

NOT shipped: the board runs #393 (patches/0071). The source is in the port
tree with the parameter defaulting to 0, so a rebuild carries it harmlessly.
